    ("Machine Learning Fundamentals", "What is the purpose of a validation set, separate from both the training and test sets?", "To tune hyperparameters and make model selection decisions without contaminating the final, unbiased evaluation on the test set", "To replace the need for a test set entirely", "To be used only after the model is already deployed", "To store backup copies of the training data", "A", "A validation set lets you make model and hyperparameter decisions iteratively, keeping the test set completely untouched to provide a final, unbiased performance estimate."),
    ("Supervised Learning", "Why does the choice of loss function matter significantly in supervised learning?", "The loss function defines what 'error' means for the specific problem, directly shaping what the model is optimized to do well at", "The loss function has no effect on model training outcomes", "All supervised learning problems must use the exact same loss function", "Loss functions are only relevant for unsupervised learning", "A", "Since training directly optimizes the chosen loss function, the specific way it defines and penalizes errors fundamentally shapes what behavior the resulting model learns."),
    ("Supervised Learning", "What potential issue can arise from training a supervised model on a dataset where the labels themselves are noisy or partially incorrect?", "The model may learn to reproduce some of that labeling noise, limiting its true achievable accuracy on correctly labeled data", "Labeling noise always improves model generalization", "Supervised learning is entirely unaffected by label quality", "This issue can only occur in regression, never classification", "A", "Since supervised models learn directly from the provided labels, systematic label noise or errors can be partially absorbed into the model, capping its real-world accuracy."),
    ("Unsupervised Learning", "Why is evaluating the quality of an unsupervised clustering result generally more challenging than evaluating a supervised classification result?", "There are no ground-truth labels to directly compare predictions against, so evaluation often relies on indirect or heuristic measures like cluster cohesion", "Unsupervised learning results can always be evaluated with simple accuracy", "Clustering algorithms never produce measurable output", "Supervised learning has no evaluation metrics either, making them equally hard", "A", "Without known correct labels to compare against, clustering quality must be assessed using indirect measures (like how tightly grouped and well-separated clusters are), which is inherently less definitive than comparing against ground truth."),
    ("Unsupervised Learning", "What does the 'curse of dimensionality' refer to in the context of unsupervised learning tasks like clustering?", "As the number of features grows very large, data points tend to become sparse and distances between them become less meaningful, making patterns harder to detect", "Adding more dimensions always makes clustering easier and faster", "It refers to a hardware limitation unrelated to data structure", "It only applies to supervised learning, never unsupervised", "A", "In very high-dimensional spaces, data points become increasingly sparse and distance-based measures (used heavily in clustering) tend to lose their discriminative power, making meaningful pattern detection harder."),
    ("Regression", "Why might adding many highly correlated (multicollinear) features to a linear regression model cause problems?", "It can make the estimated coefficients unstable and difficult to interpret, since the model struggles to attribute effects to individual correlated features", "Multicollinearity always improves prediction accuracy with no downsides", "Linear regression is mathematically immune to any feature correlation issues", "It only affects classification models, never regression", "A", "When features are highly correlated, the model has difficulty distinguishing their individual contributions, leading to unstable, hard-to-interpret coefficient estimates even if overall predictions remain reasonable."),
    ("Regression", "What is the practical effect of adding an L2 regularization penalty (Ridge regression) to a linear regression model?", "It shrinks the model's coefficients toward zero (without necessarily making them exactly zero), reducing model complexity and overfitting risk", "It always increases the magnitude of every coefficient", "It removes features from the model entirely, one at a time", "It has no effect on the resulting model at all", "A", "L2 regularization adds a penalty proportional to the squared magnitude of the coefficients, encouraging smaller (but generally non-zero) coefficient values and reducing overfitting risk."),
    ("Classification", "In a highly imbalanced binary classification problem, why might optimizing purely for accuracy lead to a practically useless model?", "A trivial model that always predicts the majority class can achieve high accuracy while providing zero value for detecting the minority class of actual interest", "Accuracy is undefined for imbalanced datasets, making this scenario impossible", "Imbalanced datasets always force a model to underfit regardless of the metric used", "This issue only affects regression problems", "A", "When one class vastly outnumbers the other, a model can achieve deceptively high accuracy by simply always predicting the majority class, while completely failing at the task's actual goal of identifying the minority class."),
    ("Classification", "What does adjusting the classification decision threshold (away from the default 0.5) typically allow you to control?", "The tradeoff between precision and recall, letting you favor one over the other depending on the application's needs", "The total size of the training dataset", "The number of features considered by the model", "The specific algorithm used for training", "A", "Moving the decision threshold shifts the balance between precision and recall - a lower threshold typically increases recall at the cost of precision, and vice versa."),
    ("KNN", "Why does KNN's prediction time typically scale poorly with a very large training dataset, compared to many other algorithms?", "A naive implementation must compute the distance from a new point to every training point at prediction time, making it computationally expensive for large datasets", "KNN has no training or prediction cost regardless of dataset size", "KNN precomputes all future predictions during training, avoiding this issue entirely", "This is a limitation only of decision trees, not KNN", "A", "Since KNN is a 'lazy learner' that defers computation to prediction time, a naive implementation must compare a new point against every stored training point, which scales poorly for large datasets."),
    ("KNN", "Why is feature scaling particularly important before applying KNN?", "Since KNN relies on distance calculations, features with larger numeric ranges can disproportionately dominate the distance metric unless all features are scaled comparably", "KNN does not use any distance calculations at all", "Feature scaling has no effect on distance-based algorithms", "KNN automatically normalizes all features internally", "A", "Because KNN's predictions depend directly on distance calculations, a feature with a much larger numeric range can dominate that calculation unless all features are put on a comparable scale first."),
    ("Decision Trees", "What does 'pruning' a decision tree typically aim to accomplish?", "Reducing the tree's size/complexity (e.g. removing branches that provide little predictive power) to help prevent overfitting", "Increasing the tree's depth indefinitely to fit training data perfectly", "Converting the tree into a neural network", "Removing all training data used to build the tree", "A", "Pruning removes branches that add little predictive value, simplifying the tree and helping it generalize better to unseen data instead of overfitting to training noise."),
    ("Decision Trees", "Why can a single, deep decision tree tend to have high variance (be very sensitive to small changes in the training data)?", "Small changes in the data can lead to very different splits early in the tree, cascading into substantially different overall tree structures", "Decision trees are mathematically guaranteed to be identical regardless of training data", "Deep trees always have lower variance than shallow trees", "Variance is not a meaningful concept for decision trees", "A", "Because early splits in a decision tree heavily shape everything below them, even small changes in the training data can lead to a different early split, cascading into a substantially different overall tree."),
    ("Naive Bayes", "Despite its strong (and often unrealistic) independence assumption, why does Naive Bayes frequently still perform well in practice for tasks like text classification?", "Even when the independence assumption is violated, the resulting class probability rankings often remain correct enough for accurate classification decisions", "The independence assumption is always exactly true for text data", "Naive Bayes secretly models feature dependencies despite its name", "It only works when features are entirely unrelated", "A", "In practice, Naive Bayes often still ranks the correct class highest even when the independence assumption doesn't perfectly hold, since classification only requires the relative ordering of probabilities to be correct, not their exact values."),
    ("Naive Bayes", "What is Laplace (additive) smoothing used for in a Naive Bayes classifier?", "To prevent zero probabilities for feature values unseen during training, which would otherwise make the entire product probability zero", "To increase the model's training speed", "To convert Naive Bayes into a decision tree", "To remove the need for any training data", "A", "Since Naive Bayes multiplies together individual feature probabilities, a single unseen feature value with zero probability would zero out the entire class probability - Laplace smoothing adds a small count to avoid this."),
    ("SVM", "How does the choice of the regularization parameter C in a soft-margin SVM affect the tradeoff between margin size and training errors?", "A larger C penalizes misclassifications more heavily (potentially a smaller margin, less tolerance for errors), while a smaller C allows a wider margin with more tolerance for misclassified points", "C only affects the kernel function used, not the margin", "C has no effect on the SVM's behavior", "A larger C always results in a simpler decision boundary", "A", "The parameter C controls how heavily misclassifications are penalized: a large C prioritizes fitting training points correctly (potentially a narrower margin), while a small C allows more margin width at the cost of tolerating more misclassifications."),
    ("SVM", "Why might an SVM with an RBF (Gaussian) kernel be preferred over a linear kernel for a dataset that is not linearly separable in its original feature space?", "The RBF kernel implicitly maps the data into a higher-dimensional space where a linear separation may become possible, without explicitly computing that transformation", "Linear kernels always outperform RBF kernels for any dataset", "RBF kernels can only be used for regression tasks", "The kernel choice has no effect on the SVM's ability to separate non-linear data", "A", "The RBF kernel implicitly projects data into a much higher-dimensional (even infinite-dimensional) space where classes that weren't linearly separable in the original space may become separable, without ever explicitly computing that transformation."),
    ("K-Means", "Why is K-Means sensitive to the initial placement of cluster centroids, and what is a common technique to mitigate this?", "Poor initialization can lead to suboptimal clustering (local minima); techniques like K-Means++ choose initial centroids more strategically to improve results", "K-Means always converges to the exact same result regardless of initialization", "Initialization has no effect on K-Means' final output", "K-Means does not use centroids at all", "A", "K-Means' iterative optimization can converge to different local minima depending on the starting centroids; K-Means++ addresses this by choosing initial centroids that are spread out, improving the odds of a good final clustering."),
    ("K-Means", "What is a key limitation of K-Means when clusters in the data have very different sizes, densities, or non-spherical shapes?", "K-Means assumes roughly spherical, similarly-sized clusters, so it can struggle to correctly separate clusters that don't fit this assumption", "K-Means can perfectly handle any cluster shape or size with no limitations", "K-Means only works when there is exactly one cluster", "Cluster shape and size never affect K-Means' results", "A", "Because K-Means assigns points based on distance to a single centroid per cluster, it implicitly assumes clusters are roughly spherical and similarly sized, and can perform poorly on data that violates this assumption."),
    ("Neural Networks", "What problem does the 'vanishing gradient' issue refer to in deep neural networks?", "Gradients can become extremely small as they are propagated backward through many layers, making it difficult for earlier layers to learn effectively", "Gradients become infinitely large during every training step", "The network's weights disappear entirely after training", "This issue only affects networks with a single layer", "A", "As gradients are propagated backward through many layers, repeated multiplication by small derivative values can cause them to shrink toward zero, leaving earlier layers with almost no meaningful learning signal."),
    ("Neural Networks", "Why is the choice of learning rate critical when training a neural network with gradient descent?", "Too high a learning rate can cause training to overshoot and diverge, while too low a learning rate can make training excessively slow or get stuck", "The learning rate has no meaningful effect on training outcomes", "A higher learning rate always guarantees faster convergence with no downsides", "Learning rate is only relevant for unsupervised learning", "A", "The learning rate controls how large each weight update step is; too large a step can overshoot good solutions and destabilize training, while too small a step can make training impractically slow or get stuck in poor local regions."),
    ("Neural Networks", "What is the purpose of using 'dropout' as a regularization technique in neural networks?", "Randomly deactivating a subset of neurons during training to prevent the network from over-relying on specific neurons, reducing overfitting", "Permanently removing neurons from the network after training completes", "Increasing the total number of neurons in every layer", "It has no effect on the network's generalization", "A", "Dropout randomly disables a fraction of neurons during each training step, preventing the network from becoming overly dependent on any specific neurons and encouraging more robust, generalizable representations."),
    ("Feature Engineering", "Why can naively applying label encoding (assigning arbitrary integers) to a categorical feature with no inherent order sometimes mislead certain models?", "Some models may incorrectly interpret the assigned numeric values as having a meaningful order or magnitude relationship that doesn't actually exist in the original categories", "Label encoding always produces perfectly accurate models regardless of the encoding scheme", "Categorical features can never be encoded numerically", "This issue only affects unsupervised learning", "A", "Assigning arbitrary integers to unordered categories can mislead models that assume numeric relationships (like linear regression), since the model may incorrectly treat category '3' as inherently 'greater than' category '1'."),
    ("Feature Engineering", "What is 'feature leakage', and why is it a serious problem in model development?", "It occurs when information from outside the legitimate training data (often related to the target) inadvertently influences training, leading to unrealistically optimistic performance that won't hold up in production", "It refers to a model losing accuracy over time due to hardware failure", "It is a synonym for regularization", "It only affects unsupervised learning tasks", "A", "Feature leakage happens when information that wouldn't genuinely be available at prediction time (often correlated with or derived from the target) sneaks into training, inflating apparent performance in a way that won't hold up on truly new data."),
